    "body": "## Problem\n\nWith three linked ranges on one line, changing a word's length can corrupt the other occurrences. For example, deleting the first character of the last word in `hello hello hello` produces `ello helloello` instead of `ello ello ello`.\n\n`update_ranges()` applies replacements from left to right using a snapshot of positions. Earlier replacements shift the later ranges before they are edited.\n\n## Solution\n\nApply replacements in reverse buffer order. Add coverage for shortening and lengthening each of three same-line ranges, including undo/redo. Update the test server to return current positions after edits.\n\n## Validation\n\nAll six regressions fail without the fix; the complete linked-editing spec passes with it. Full Lua formatting, runtime type checking and commit lint pass. Linux Debug: 10,192 functional tests and 822 unit tests pass, with 77 functional and 6 unit skips. Five functional failures also reproduce on the unpatched base: two temporary-directory cases, server endpoint parsing and two Tutor highlighting cases.\n\nAI-assisted.\n",
    "closed_at": null,
